{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he shall bring her offering.</b> It is impossible for this offering to atone for her, for the sacrifice of the wicked is an abomination. Rather, the man brings an offering as atonement for having seen in her some looseness at first and not reproved her, thereby causing the honored and awesome Name to be blotted out in the waters." "<b>Groats of barley meal.</b> Just as it is all ground mixed together, like animal fodder — he shall not pour oil on it and shall not put frankincense on it. The oil and frankincense in other meal-offerings are called אזכרה because the one who brings them is remembered through his meal-offering; but this meal-offering shall be <b>a reminder of iniquity</b>, as it proceeds to explain, for it is a meal-offering of jealousy. Another interpretation: <b>he shall not pour, and shall not put, for it is a meal-offering of jealousies</b> — meaning that it contains nothing but a spirit of jealousy." "<b>A reminder of iniquity.</b> One of two: either the sin of the woman or the sin of the husband who suspects her." "<b>For it is a meal-offering of jealousies.</b> The first is masculine gender in shureq, which refers to the meal — מנחת קנאות in patakh (i.e., קְנָאֹת, masculine). The second מנחת קנאות is in ḥirik (i.e., קְנָאִת), which refers to the meal-offering itself, which is feminine gender."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:16:37.766446799Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}